  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1038 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nAs an upper low over northern California churns slowly east on \nFriday, deeper southwest flow over the Desert Southwest will carry a \nshortwave trough across New Mexico. This process will send a band \nof monsoon moisture toward the TX/NM border by the evening complete \nwith some low PoPs. These rain chances improve considerably by \nSaturday afternoon and especially the evening as the axis of \nmoisture overspreads the Caprock within broad troughing. This \nabundance of background ascent with PWAT anomalies pushing +0.8\" and \npotential for training precip in deep SW flow suggests some heavy \nrain and flooding could redevelop and continue overnight. Additional \ndeep convection is possible Sunday, but this time favoring areas off \nthe Caprock along and east of the upper trough axis. Models have \nsadly rejected a cold front for Sunday, so this keeps highs well \nabove normal all weekend. Provided this cold front reaches our \narea on Monday, more PoPs are justified even with weak ridging \naloft on the heels of Sunday's trough. The upper flow then backs \nmore southwesterly as the monsoonal fetch deepens courtesy of a \nlow/trough over SOCAL. Ensembles favor a cool and wet pattern\nthrough Wednesday, although there is concern for much of this \nmoisture to hang up farther west within deeper meridional flow. \nThis implies PoPs in later forecasts may exhibit a sharper \ngradient from W-E either in or somewhere near our forecast area.",
